**Partial dent in HATCH 1.** Fixed prime-power weights and direct Galois folding both face a provable obstruction. Adaptive selection from the public residue table remains open.

**Multiplicity — proved.** Theorem J extends to a separate nonnegative weight at every depth \(q^k\). Even granting every weighted Johnson certificate at unit cost, a profile fixed before seeing the interval yields at most \(\rho(\log X/\log B)^{1-o(1)}\) smooth witnesses per call on average. Reweighting can certify an individual witness below the unweighted Johnson threshold, but the leading gain over Dickman-rate testing is zero. [Proof and hypotheses](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/hatch_h1c/report.md:9)

**Folding — proved.** At a good split prime, a primitive quadratic norm vanishes on exactly one of two conjugate branches. Requiring both components to vanish gives zero agreement. A low-degree polynomial vanishing on every admissible folded symbol must contain the known norm factor modulo that prime. Keeping either branch gives a two-choice projective decoding problem. [Folding argument](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/hatch_h1c/report.md:32)

**Measured.** Across six fixtures each at 40, 64, 80 and 96 bits, the interval lattice found **25 of 119** smooth integers. Quadratic rational reconstruction found **65 of 80** witnesses and returned exactly the candidates from the matched divisor lattices. At 96 bits it took **5.96 ms per witness**, versus **10.27 ms** for the full lattice sieve, with only three reconstruction hits. All 48 fixture lists were empty at one quarter of their respective Johnson benchmarks. [Measurements](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/hatch_h1c/report.md:42) · [Agreement curves](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/hatch_h1c/agreement.pdf)

**Exponent ledger.** The measured speedup comes from recovering a point faster once its divisor is chosen. The implemented traversal still visits \(L_N[1-\beta,2\nu]\) divisor products at leading order under M2’s split-prime assumption. A selector finding one useful witness in \(n^a\) work would conditionally give \(L_N[\beta,\max((1+a)\kappa,2\kappa)]\), including relation collection and sparse linear algebra. Selecting the branch *before* enumerating those products is the unresolved step toward \(\beta=1/4\). [Ledger](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/hatch_h1c/report.md:55)